#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199281} :activity/id #uuid "3436fa6e-7d06-5a5e-90bb-b6107d2ecf0e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048185 :topic/id #uuid "8698dcb0-d732-5f62-8605-520598e45185" :topic/math-academy-id 4145} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198378 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198020 :question/id #uuid "9a1112c0-db94-5572-ba53-b78116b7c273" :question/math-academy-id "e-12355"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9b46ce4-cfe0-5da6-bc91-65ae8c544393" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050508}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501152}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Through a Surface as a Double Integral"} :step/id #uuid "a6470e60-331b-571e-b3d2-ff5a04e501fb" :step/math-academy-id 44278 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199283}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199465 :tutorial/content "Recall that the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ across a smooth, orientable surface $S$ with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S  \\mathbf F \\cdot {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} \\,\\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}}$ is a unit normal vector that defines the orientation of the surface.\n\n![](images/9a/9aa63a1e4f1d278cef6c53600e8fb9c60829de972ba7409afdc32837ecbc3e07.png)\n\nIf $S$ is given by a parametric equation of the form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v), \\qquad (u,v)\\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,\n$$\n\nwe can determine the unit normal vector using the fundamental product as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{n}} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v}{\\| \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\|}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting this into the formula for the flux, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\frac{{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}}{‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot \\frac{{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}}{‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖}\\,‖{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $S$ is a smooth, orientable surface given in parametric form by $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ then the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D  \\mathbf F(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "32db1cfe-d870-502c-a709-0a79ee70a616" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7633 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "36b6d02c-b1f4-58b5-b15a-0a072e1d5cac" :step/math-academy-id 44280 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198380 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198022 :question/id #uuid "0364a11f-e567-517e-acf2-471708dbdc0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12358"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f322cca7-7530-50bf-b262-89faa81f5906" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434831}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Flux Through a Surface"} :step/id #uuid "d2e640e5-2cd0-57f1-8576-f38b3f6df4a4" :step/math-academy-id 44283}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Parametric Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198487}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434834} :activity/id #uuid "67831f4f-8138-536f-9514-23aeb5868445" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048185 :topic/id #uuid "8698dcb0-d732-5f62-8605-520598e45185" :topic/math-academy-id 4145} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434832 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434830 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b1374a-677b-550b-816a-6cbd02c5f061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434818} {:answer/id #uuid "29ead56c-2949-5fb0-8895-cdd6a87df4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434820} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3bf5e7-fb1f-5845-863a-15bfb2564e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434824} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e03b7e-e933-5e07-a534-f79399c89baf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434825} {:answer/id #uuid "c757d8e1-3c0b-5fc9-9360-e929a186b1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434820} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1855324-ee18-529c-ba15-53c93565cb17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22bda1b7-731c-5be9-8f1b-6668d9e068b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161253" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,xz,\\,yz⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨u,\\,{u}^{2},\\,v⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}auv+b{u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2u,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2u \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,xz,\\,yz⟩{|}_{x=u\\,y={u}^{2}\\,z=v} \\\\  & =⟨v,\\,uv,\\,{u}^{2}v⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨v,\\,uv,\\,{u}^{2}v⟩\\cdot ⟨2u,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =v\\cdot 2u+uv\\cdot (-1)+{u}^{2}v\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2uv-uv+0 \\\\  & =uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1\\cdot uv+0\\cdot {u}^{2}v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1+0=1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "764dfadc-7353-5f72-bb5e-9db6eccbfeca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434833}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434833 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434831 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4ed277-51d3-5190-9f15-e73dd884cf47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434819} {:answer/id #uuid "471dabbc-d133-52dc-b559-394801c709a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434821} {:answer/id #uuid "777af6b2-9aef-5099-aac7-98b1ec37120e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434822} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1f231c-84b6-5409-b4c5-4c9196b18b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434823} {:answer/id #uuid "de47629f-21da-52b1-941b-ae2f3625cecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434827}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434819} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a1a0c12-6b57-5589-b4e1-416cdc457a68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5815497-10bb-5d31-b1ef-c78b6eb79a17" :question/math-academy-id "q-161259" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,y,\\,-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u+v,\\,v-u,\\,uv⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 0,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-u-v,\\,v-u,\\,2⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-u-v,\\,v-u,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & ={⟨x,\\,y,\\,-z⟩|}_{x=u+v\\,y=v-u\\,z=uv} \\\\  & =⟨u+v,\\,v-u,\\,-uv⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u+v,\\,v-u,\\,-uv⟩\\cdot ⟨-u-v,\\,v-u,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =(u+v)\\cdot (-u-v)+(v-u)\\cdot (v-u)+(-uv)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-{u}^{2}-2uv-{v}^{2}+{v}^{2}-2uv+{u}^{2}-2uv \\\\  & =-6uv\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x,\\,y,\\,-z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-6uv)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}uv\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,\\text{d}v{\\int }_{-2}^{0}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-6\\cdot [\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9468f911-b5bd-557b-9a6f-8e025c4a65c6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194750cf-4b33-5153-97c1-bd1b0f21c3c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8u{v}^{3}+8{u}^{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275713} {:answer/id #uuid "30772c46-8461-5daa-94aa-ff5b8a754f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8u{v}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275909} {:answer/id #uuid "45100deb-1ec8-5bbb-b0b4-3c26c5616332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2u{v}^{3}+4{u}^{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276086} {:answer/id #uuid "45e6001c-4deb-5a8c-b01f-01531e4489d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4u{v}^{3}+2{u}^{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276094} {:answer/id #uuid "936bab95-9822-587d-80ac-54eded632448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{u}^{3}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186276811} :answer-field/id #uuid "f81d9840-6037-5557-81d1-599235b0ea5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9fcf8673-a62b-588a-8020-ca78007689b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160840" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v),$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨2x+y,\\,2z,\\,y+z⟩,\\,r(u,v)=⟨-{u}^{2},\\,2{u}^{2}-{v}^{2},\\,{v}^{2}⟩,\\,(u,v)\\in D⊂{R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨8uv,\\,4uv,\\,4uv⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNotice we are told that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨8uv,\\,4uv,\\,4uv⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2x+y,\\,2z,\\,y+z⟩{|}_{x=-{u}^{2}\\,y=2{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}\\,z={v}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨2(-{u}^{2})+(2{u}^{2}-{v}^{2}),\\,2({v}^{2}),\\,(2{u}^{2}-{v}^{2})+{v}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{v}^{2},\\,2{v}^{2},\\,2{u}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-{v}^{2},\\,2{v}^{2},\\,2{u}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨8uv,\\,4uv,\\,4uv⟩ \\\\  & =(-{v}^{2})\\cdot 8uv+2{v}^{2}\\cdot 4uv+2{u}^{2}\\cdot 4uv \\\\  & =-8u{v}^{3}+8u{v}^{3}+8{u}^{3}v \\\\  & =8{u}^{3}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,8{u}^{3}v\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e88e2f68-fd58-50c1-9108-060ce4b3171a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434832}}] :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Parametric Surfaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434817}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130278} :activity/id #uuid "edadecea-96b5-58a0-937e-a3136a7e0eb3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048452 :topic/id #uuid "a0ae7c05-7b28-571b-907d-73a833a9a41a" :topic/math-academy-id 2087} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130393 :tutorial/content "We know how to solve a system of differential equations when they're decoupled. However, most systems are not decoupled. So, how do we solve them?\n\nLet's consider a general linear system of two differential equations:\n\n![](images/72/72a4d6a819a56d80818184e16205028a3a51eab2e97d1cdad72b8d495e5fd8d1.svg)\n\nIt turns out that if the matrix $A$ has two *distinct real eigenvalues* $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, then $A$ is diagonalizable and we can solve the system.\n\nIf the eigenvectors corresponding to $\\color{red}\\lambda_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ are ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ respectively, then the general solution to our original system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThis result is true for any $n \\times n$ system with $n$ distinct eigenvalues. We'll prove this result for a $2\\times 2$ system at the end of the lesson.\n\nNow, let's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "ffec0b5f-817b-58dc-b521-20db431e3ea6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4183 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fd45b863-00a5-58fa-8428-a9a8120bd4ea" :step/math-academy-id 540572 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129305 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128982 :question/id #uuid "c7bde1c8-25e8-5573-9ac4-feedd40e122f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5974"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16d1e71b-6eda-5c91-81a0-15a1aa979932" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049852}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413715}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution Given the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of the Corresponding Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "c550f4f1-3cf8-524d-8169-2af08dc4a285" :step/math-academy-id 540573 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129306 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128952 :question/id #uuid "58c1b360-e67c-5074-919b-18fb5dbab146" :question/math-academy-id "e-5606"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cfc838b-2e21-5021-8afc-000d12acdf39" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413716}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a Linear System of Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "8e58211c-b5cb-545e-80a7-711cd519848c" :step/math-academy-id 540576 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130281}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130359 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to solve a decoupled system of differential equations. Our goal now is to understand in more detail why this method works. \n\nLet's consider our general linear system of two differential equations once again:\n\n![](images/65/6529692bc6317e2c675e756533a733c0caf087f3ac0b64b9c3a407924014d94b.svg)\n\nWe will show that if the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, we can convert it into an equivalent decoupled system.\n\nIf $A$ is diagonalizable, it has $2$ linearly independent eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$ We use these to form the *change of variables matrix* $E,$ given by\n\n![](images/c1/c11833cbccd43c172ab7699a9000df5e2cf4bfedb0720958f1d5a407e5aeb8f2.svg)\n\nBecause the columns are linearly independent, $E$ is invertible. Then, the substitution  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwill transform our original system of differential equations into a decoupled one, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(Eu(t)) & =A(Eu(t)) \\\\ E{u}^{′}(t) & =AEu(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={E}^{-1}AE\\,u(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n![](images/c9/c9657a197b8232d883c6da546170754f2cc6916378d0c269755b3b5aae2173e0.svg)\n\nis a diagonal matrix, and ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1},$ ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ are the eigenvalues of $A$ corresponding to ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$\n\nFollowing these steps, we now have the *decoupled system*\n![](images/55/557b8775c900635024b99ba9123d8023b2667db0459b1c8df53df886f4b156f8.svg)\n\nHere, the matrix $D = E^{-1}$ is sometimes called the **decoupling matrix**.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "375f9ca0-335a-5289-80aa-053eba96a1f3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3650 :tutorial/title "Decoupling a Linear System of Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "5f88f9eb-8194-50fe-b904-394d292db648" :step/math-academy-id 540579 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130282}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130282 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129307 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128951 :question/id #uuid "b0707ce1-a0fe-5749-9742-4768621332a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5605"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a962d5e-5cc9-530e-9f95-85c399af7e4b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049678}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392306}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Decoupling Substitution of a Linear System of Differential Equations"} :step/id #uuid "bdfa89d7-8a8f-598a-98e1-507a34d62369" :step/math-academy-id 540580 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130283}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130394 :tutorial/content "Consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that $A$ is diagonalizable and has eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ with corresponding eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2},$ respectively.\n\nWe've been using the general solution formula \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? First, remember that the substitution\n\n![](images/7e/7ece1267196b62dfffdf40b16af1b94e9169b6a798f7841f0fd361733042764f.svg)\n\ntransforms the matrix differential equation into the equivalent decoupled system\n\n![](images/a6/a61af1176fb00e767ce5a8fc0ce349b79742937909f70c01701db56b126b010e.svg)\n\nWe can solve this new decoupled system using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $\\mathbf{x}(t)=E\\mathbf{u}(t)$, we can transform the system back to the variable $\\mathbf x(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =Eu(t) \\\\  & =E({c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}E[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}E[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our original system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "91036007-f4d2-5e97-ac9f-760e367f3217" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4184 :tutorial/title "The Proof of the Formula For a General System"} :step/id #uuid "e1277628-257e-5f4b-942e-3126a1882b65" :step/math-academy-id 540583}] :activity/title "Solving Homogeneous Systems of ODEs With Distinct Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129398}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172908} :activity/id #uuid "1cf8d435-d537-5b08-8592-19448de0ebaa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048656 :topic/id #uuid "b2c975cf-4aab-5305-b223-4eaed44fd857" :topic/math-academy-id 3338} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173162 :tutorial/content "Consider the unit circle configured in the usual way in the coordinate plane. Suppose a particle $P$ is initially at the point $(1,0)$ and moves counterclockwise along the unit circle with a fixed **angular velocity** of $\\dfrac\\pi3$ **radians per second.** \n\nThe first three seconds of the particle's motion are illustrated below.\n\n![](images/00/009b83d5d0e48c6983950a48523838cc569b3a92511dc9362630316b1d88f86b.png)\n\n    \n\nSince the angular velocity is constant, we refer to this as **uniform circular motion**. This fixed angular velocity is denoted $\\omega,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac\\pi 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf r(t)$ be the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIt's worth building a picture of why this formula is true:\n\n- We know that $\\mathbf r(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf i + \\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf j$ traces out a unit circle, starting at the point $(1,0).$\n\n- We can see from the picture that $P$ will return to the starting position after $t=6$ seconds, so the angular frequency of the motion is\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ indeed returns to its starting location when $t=6{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(6) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,j \\\\  & =r(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if a particle moves counterclockwise along a circle of radius $R$ centered at $O$ with initial position $(R,0),$ then its position vector $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\!\\,.\n$$\n\nNote that for clockwise motion, we have $\\omega < 0.$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a0eebf3c-594c-5d34-868e-6c476f587498" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5092 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fd3fa277-366a-51f9-a9dd-222993535dab" :step/math-academy-id 31935 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172913}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172044 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171850 :question/id #uuid "dd9a6e6a-e3d1-5684-8a14-7da410461aad" :question/math-academy-id "e-8642"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb1745c5-6485-5a9d-9e8f-19492928e4b4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501167}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Position Function of an Object Moving With Circular Motion"} :step/id #uuid "392b768a-c4a0-5b11-884b-71eb53759374" :step/math-academy-id 32081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172045 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171851 :question/id #uuid "cfcca273-3b96-5812-8b0d-029875f0585a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8643"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d9865d-2e8e-59fe-9a81-8935df037303" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050988}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362226} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414224}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Velocity and Speed of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"} :step/id #uuid "c1002b1a-1dd1-5a5c-addc-3f3958efbc84" :step/math-academy-id 32082 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173094}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172046 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171852 :question/id #uuid "5614ccb1-e2be-5944-8229-61d62e5860fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8644"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17787055-19de-5dca-b1f9-e97a3a7c2aff" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050614}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362234} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501234}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration of a Particle Moving With Circular Motion"} :step/id #uuid "3cf60778-9584-5fa1-a983-0b811161a21e" :step/math-academy-id 32083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173205 :tutorial/content "Suppose a particle $P$ moves along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\n    \n\nNote the following:\n\n- The **position vector** $\\mathbf r$ extends from the origin to $P$ in the *radial* direction, while\n\n- the **velocity vector** $\\mathbf v$ points in the direction of the particle's motion, *tangential* to the circle.\n\nFirst, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nWe can easily derive a convenient formula relating $\\mathbf v$ and $\\omega$ by differentiating $\\mathbf r(t){:}$\n\nDifferentiating once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & ={r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={(R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j))}^{′} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the magnitude of this vector, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||v|{|}^{2} & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t)^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t)}^{2}) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\omega t+{cos}^{2}⁡\\omega t) \\\\  & ={R}^{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf v || = R|\\omega|.\n$$\n\nNote that while the magnitude of the velocity vector (i.e., the speed) is constant, its direction is continuously changing, which means that an object in uniform circular motion also experiences *acceleration* (more on this shortly)." :tutorial/id #uuid "04a3ea53-9923-588e-b205-6980447b9231" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5805 :tutorial/title "Linear vs. Angular Velocity"} :step/id #uuid "b328c2b2-b79d-5bf1-9c7d-2b99b0ab3382" :step/math-academy-id 35574 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173094 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173260 :tutorial/content "Let's return to our particle $P$ moving along a circle of radius $R$ centered at the origin in the $xy$ plane. \n\n![](images/45/4564f4c4fcd8068f3bf1645a310afab7fcdc2f82fec6966cb220558c1e93639a.png)\n\n    \n\nOnce again, let's assume that $P$ is at the point $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get the velocity vector $\\mathbf v{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf v(t) =R\\omega\\left(-\\sin\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\n$$\n\nDifferentiating once more, we get an expression for the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =R\\omega {(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,j)}^{′} \\\\  & =R\\omega (-\\omega \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i-\\omega \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}\\cdot R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{\\omega }^{2}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a = -\\omega^2 \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration vector acts in the *opposite* direction to $\\mathbf r.$ In other words, $\\mathbf a$ points *toward the center of the circle*. Moreover, we have\n\n$$\n||\\mathbf a|| = \\omega^2 || \\mathbf r ||.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d24aa67f-988c-52f1-89ef-bb9571593fa1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7810 :tutorial/title "The Acceleration Vector"} :step/id #uuid "f5f2037c-c390-5f31-b9b4-856ee301062a" :step/math-academy-id 45177 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172915}}] :activity/title "Circular Motion About the Origin" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172168}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501261} :activity/id #uuid "948eb367-d5fd-51df-b125-e3149e49d0c4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048656 :topic/id #uuid "b2c975cf-4aab-5305-b223-4eaed44fd857" :topic/math-academy-id 3338} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501261 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172655 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06716dfe-08a6-516e-a629-177bb828c2fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=-3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500479} {:answer/id #uuid "31e995dd-ff4a-5e6d-8cf4-3bfb96a6017b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500571} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2848b3-8ddd-5fb7-95ae-348cf52cb0ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\pi t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500591} {:answer/id #uuid "be530a2a-8d30-591a-8cf9-2082ebebbbb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500863} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a4ff08-122d-5fd6-b066-879bdb9fa494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\pi t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500978}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500571} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa2448a5-0101-5a28-982e-8f121a6f37a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "297d89f1-479f-5e7e-834d-0ffc08d592cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-97586" :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $2\\pi \\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(3,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(3,0),$ we have that $r(0)=\\,3i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=3.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=3$ and $\\omega =-2\\pi \\text{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi t)\\,j) \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "368577ca-f476-5cfa-9e6b-273c753b6b1a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501282}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501234 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a682535-8c56-55dd-a035-f6b7cc19fc38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "54\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500486} {:answer/id #uuid "6f323c8b-a972-54d0-bbd1-34949e7b25d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500694} {:answer/id #uuid "7be1cdf9-0f94-5f70-8486-f5416dfcc558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "81\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500724} {:answer/id #uuid "98ff3c6b-5897-5008-9dfd-a379593dd6c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500779} {:answer/id #uuid "a385d26b-a169-54eb-af49-c35e70069687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500796} :answer-field/id #uuid "4264e826-1eca-5166-bc99-fe720168444a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea3775f0-4a19-53d1-b4e1-4eb3518e099f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97670" :question/problem "An object moves along a circular path with position $r(t)=3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j)$ meters. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the object at time $t,$ measured in seconds." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nFirst, we find the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(r(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(3(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j))^{′} \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =(v(t))^{′} \\\\  & =(-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,i+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,j)^{′} \\\\  & =-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t\\,i-27\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the magnitude of the acceleration as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||a(t)|| & =\\sqrt{(-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)^{2}+(-27\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{27}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡3t+{27}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡3t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{27}^{2}} \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude of the acceleration is $27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nNote that $||a||$ is constant, but $a$ is not. \n\n**Method 2**\n\nFor uniform circular motion in the $xy$-plane, we have\n\n$$\na=-{\\omega }^{2}r\\,⇒\\,||a||={\\omega }^{2}||r||.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius of the circle is $a=3\\,\\text{m}$ and the angular frequency $\\omega =3\\,\\text{rad/s}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n||a||={3}^{2}\\cdot 3=27\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5c6597e4-8215-5ac5-9944-f088aeed0bde"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501167 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05373043-ede4-5f3b-ab00-6c5eb28bd928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500476} {:answer/id #uuid "43f6eebb-f90b-5e3e-bf7f-5a7b4d0e7ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500601} {:answer/id #uuid "510f8649-056c-567e-b3ee-8b8aa7b327bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500633} {:answer/id #uuid "9cde80f5-0a17-51cc-8fcb-e28702d3897c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500785} {:answer/id #uuid "e33da50a-6797-5e61-8dad-558084c1c0d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500950}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500950} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f20aec0-879d-5670-bfea-c9c0def60bdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b5c204f-7839-5ec6-95c0-a9151439158a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97637" :question/problem "A particle moves along a **clockwise** circular path with center $O$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(1,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(1,0),$ we have that $r(0)=\\,1i,$ and we can conclude that the radius of the circle $R=1.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=1$ and $\\omega =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\text{ rad/s}$ (negative because the motion is clockwise), we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7068fb21-64e6-51f4-b3a5-0162c383a600" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501282 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cd02180-7f22-5f61-93c7-fff08cd0fa7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360952} {:answer/id #uuid "3ad8a471-2d04-5600-9388-bfbdf3391f3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361105} {:answer/id #uuid "75a3c33c-0bca-5f44-a5b5-84ee07be1813" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361356} {:answer/id #uuid "845ab6b4-5c02-5255-a705-c026ab5d4e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361427} {:answer/id #uuid "b0e84a90-a66f-5f65-ad44-888c40336abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360952} :answer-field/id #uuid "87b5905c-bd9f-57b9-8aca-d92b2a486288" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4a58d6f2-a102-5329-9df6-5ca2570034eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-97631" :question/problem "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $1\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(4,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(4,0),$ we have that $r(0)=4i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=4.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=4$ and $\\omega =1\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b298bba9-5caf-53be-a1b8-5e5bfe3389e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029a7f99-f667-5979-a59f-0e86cfa31195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360840} {:answer/id #uuid "5407564b-be54-5b7e-b365-08c7dd2979bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361209} {:answer/id #uuid "aed4d295-17ee-5234-8841-738be0c227d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361610} {:answer/id #uuid "ba462204-6404-53bb-a653-40fd5d5fc0bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361667} {:answer/id #uuid "fada0c54-2f8f-5502-bc13-443d0123345e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "r(t)=4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡8t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡8t\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361610} :answer-field/id #uuid "bff7f1a0-ee8f-51c5-b306-5103d68b521c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362157}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0696e9be-8b02-58ba-8734-5aa777937265" :question/math-academy-id "q-97632" :question/problem "A particle moves along a circular path centered at $O$ in the $xy$-plane in the counterclockwise direction with a constant angular velocity of $4\\text{ rad/s}.$ Given that the object is at the position $(8,0)$ when $t=0,$ find the position function of the particle." :question/worked-solution "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $R$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(R,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nSince the object's initial position is $(8,0),$ we have that $r(0)=8i,$ and we can conclude that the circle's radius is $R=8.$\n\nTherefore, since we have $R=8$ and $\\omega =4\\text{ rad/s},$ we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c62d5c7d-4f34-5f60-92b7-d492ba362088" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501272}}] :activity/title "Circular Motion About the Origin" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500464}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199334} :activity/id #uuid "e864b1f7-a24d-5b18-b4a7-c727177eb578" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049333 :topic/id #uuid "ec9aa34d-69be-5e48-a2c9-562d23408d0d" :topic/math-academy-id 4167} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199331 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198408 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198050 :question/id #uuid "cb13bd5e-ec8a-5e5c-a45f-d710ced8c3ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-12473"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b2c4733-27b9-52be-b326-b4360580b685" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050326}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501161}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of a Rod About the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "482d14bd-b9c9-5f57-b73c-1c1f49d74d92" :step/math-academy-id 44913 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199347}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199332 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198409 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198051 :question/id #uuid "4ebe8c87-95ba-58e2-b5d4-1bb19f231dc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12474"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "56762142-e975-5cd2-8699-3bf7a9605e93" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417068}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Center of Mass of a Rod"} :step/id #uuid "af9ac3a2-f3c4-5021-867e-f5efa7e918da" :step/math-academy-id 44914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199333 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198410 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198052 :question/id #uuid "5c469b72-7ee3-5084-be63-2b7e62b42146" :question/math-academy-id "e-12475"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70278fe3-d8dd-5f72-8bf7-f1a4a17be526" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417066}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Mass of a Thin Rod"} :step/id #uuid "7574e535-619b-5fb0-b185-be9a15178e66" :step/math-academy-id 44915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199346}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199334 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199486 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to determine the center of mass of a thin rod whose mass is unevenly distributed. \n\nWe describe the mass distribution along a thin rod using a so-called **mass density function** $\\lambda (x).$ This function tells us the *mass per unit length* at any point $x$ on the rod. The units of $\\lambda$ are usually $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{m}.$\n\nConsider a thin rod between the points $x=1$ and $x=3$ along the $x$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c9ee70d28476ebda61ccce5c6342a7f1a48a3e0f4dc26ee1af147d6f3d34a218.png)\n\n \n\nSuppose the mass density function of the rod is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda(x) = 3x^2 \\, \\textrm{kg/m},\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is measured in meters. What is the total mass of the rod?\n\nTo answer this, consider a point $x \\in (1,3)$ on the rod and cut out a small piece of length $\\Delta{x},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9b62c29b499159fe23e9ece7023ed179f4565600a88677fe51ab002a4a0d6faf.png)\n\n \n\nIf $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, we can assume that our density function is approximately constant over the selected piece. As a result, the mass of this piece is\n\n$$\n\\Delta{m} \\approx 3x^2 \\Delta{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\Delta{m}$ are \n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\textrm{m} = \\textrm{kg},$\nas we'd expect.\n\nTo find the total mass of the rod, we add together the masses of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta x \\to 0.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\nm =  \\int_{1}^{3} 3x^2 \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nCarrying out the integration, we find that the total mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & ={3}^{3}-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =26\\,\\text{kg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given a thin rod oriented along the $x$-axis over the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b,$ if $\\lambda(x)$ denotes the rod's mass density function, then the total mass of the rod is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\int_a^b \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3d97cfb9-13c8-5638-8080-e1c8cd1b90f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7760 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "57e92bfb-7b99-5c98-ae8c-4999964eb518" :step/math-academy-id 44917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199333}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199346 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199490 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw how to calculate the total moment about the origin of a collection of masses distributed along the $x$-axis. We'll now extend our understanding to include moments of thin rods.\n\nConsider a thin rod between the points $x=1$ and $x=3$ along the $x$-axis with mass density function $\\lambda(x) = 3x^2 \\, \\textrm{kg/m},$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c9/c9ee70d28476ebda61ccce5c6342a7f1a48a3e0f4dc26ee1af147d6f3d34a218.png)\n\nHow can we find the total moment of the rod about the origin?\n\nTo answer this, we once again consider a small piece of rod located in the interval $(x, x+\\Delta x),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd2dc58c503333a365b140de3ef1b559477788953dab14549478ca031df98125.png)\n\n \n\nIf $\\Delta{x}$ is very small, we can approximate this piece as a point mass. \n\nThe moment of this piece about the origin, denoted $\\Delta{M_O},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{M}_{O} & ≈x\\cdot Δm \\\\  & ≈x\\cdot \\lambda (x)Δx \\\\  & =x\\cdot 3{x}^{2}Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, to find the moment of the entire rod about the origin, we sum the moments of all such pieces and take the limit as $\\Delta x.$ By doing so, we get \n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_{1}^{3} 3x^3 \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral, we find that the total moment about $O$ of the rod is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(3)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3(1)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =60\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn general, the moment about the origin of a rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ for $a \\leq x \\leq b$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_O = \\int_a^b x\\, \\lambda(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "23310936-fde8-5269-917c-4aec2c5591cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7776 :tutorial/title "The Moment of a Rod About the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "459d7e8d-a9ee-5771-a2c6-38ae023189cb" :step/math-academy-id 45028 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199331}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199347 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199491 :tutorial/content "A thin rod's **center of mass** is the point $\\bar{x}$ on the $x$-axis where the system's total mass could be concentrated to give the same moment as the original rod. \n\nAnalogous to the case of point masses, the center of mass of a thin rod with mass density function $\\lambda(x)$ placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{M_O}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\displaystyle m = \\int_a^b \\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the mass of the rod, and\n\n- $\\displaystyle M_O = \\int_a^b \\, x\\lambda (x) \\: \\textrm{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "05cbd84c-3413-5883-bf92-6da730395e8e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7777 :tutorial/title "The Center of Mass of a Rod"} :step/id #uuid "a8a2d02d-5f5b-5ea7-8825-3d994a093207" :step/math-academy-id 45029 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199332}}] :activity/title "Moments and Centers of Mass of Thin Rods" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198496}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180501} :activity/id #uuid "912b55f4-8877-5aff-bc43-d73698914e66" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049346 :topic/id #uuid "ed9d339c-fde4-545b-86b4-381d2f85203f" :topic/math-academy-id 3704} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180678 :tutorial/content "Since there is a fundamental link between line integrals of vector-valued functions and line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y,$ it should be unsurprising that they share similar properties. We list two of them below:\n\n- If $-C$ denotes the path obtained by reversing the orientation of $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\n- If $C = C_1\\cup C_2$ is the union of two paths $C_1$ and $C_2$, then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r \n= \n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nThe first property has an intuitive meaning if we think about it in terms of the work done by a force on a particle. To see this, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ shown below. Let $C$ be the path defined by traversing the line segment from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4f/4f69f80d98d301410908f7ec867a89bdc834c22a51f224d9b00b80ceca39ab87.png)\n\n \n\nLet $W(C)$ be the work done in moving the particle along $C.$ Since our particle moves in the same direction as the vector field, we have\n\n$$\nW(C) = \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if we move the particle from $B$ to $A,$ we expect the line integral to be negative. This is because the particle is now moving against $\\mathbf F.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fe6b2e67d33a4993104406c88b1dc0dc897be33d034007ed581a75f741ddf620.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW(-C) = \\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$\n\nWe can show that the work done in moving the particle along $-C$ is the same as the work done in moving it along $C$ but with the opposite sign. That is\n\n$$\nW(-C) = -W(C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d745eed3-7e69-55af-b685-1c7482dfffb9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3976 :tutorial/title "Reversing the Orientation of a Curve and Sums Over Unions of Paths"} :step/id #uuid "b7ff2ff0-c95d-5155-89fb-bfd7eb079783" :step/math-academy-id 23622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179524 :question/id #uuid "734d74ca-26cf-57d3-b3dc-fec66d7e0264" :question/math-academy-id "e-6286"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424621}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"} :step/id #uuid "5214fa4f-2dd0-5f35-827a-aa639d600260" :step/math-academy-id 23623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179527 :question/id #uuid "2e6103cc-7be6-5eb6-ab5a-aa5aff3fa423" :question/math-academy-id "e-6319"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050687}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424620}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"} :step/id #uuid "6acb0f4b-5b56-5e4c-a728-4db2fd840bff" :step/math-academy-id 23747 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180387}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179600 :question/id #uuid "171fef73-d124-5aca-ad06-9f20ae5a087c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424622}] :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"} :step/id #uuid "c7465601-d18a-5a90-bfe9-a27fe52c871f" :step/math-academy-id 35726 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180386}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180722 :tutorial/content "Line integrals of vector-valued functions share a fundamental connection with line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y.$\n\nConsider a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with components $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $C$ is a smooth curve on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r (t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [a,b].\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the line integral of $\\mathbf F$ along $C$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)  \\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nAn analogous result holds for vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3.$ That is, \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d y + R(x,y,z) \\,\\textrm d z\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4ca8cae-81df-5c55-90ac-839b063e78a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5837 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4246c091-bba6-587e-8d43-72e5c01a1e08" :step/math-academy-id 35769 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180492}}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179857}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424625} :activity/id #uuid "c1a363ff-d156-5c04-b913-8176faf9c905" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049346 :topic/id #uuid "ed9d339c-fde4-545b-86b4-381d2f85203f" :topic/math-academy-id 3704} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf6d6b3-ad5e-5dc6-8ebb-24366de741cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424604} {:answer/id #uuid "90b2b589-9e4a-50b5-aade-f9285dbfd609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}2{e}^{x+y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424608} {:answer/id #uuid "91f4495b-123f-57a5-9de2-b6a7f1714f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424609} {:answer/id #uuid "adc05a61-f7ae-5052-9af4-0da99b3ef9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{2(x+y+z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424611} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa1700d-0795-5ad2-aa9c-b22297bf2101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424604} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef1db59-b0fd-505e-b25a-7a9041ecb1ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7d70870-3ade-5c38-9cda-96260cf64575" :question/math-academy-id "q-125414" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)={e}^{y+z}\\,i+{e}^{x+z}\\,j+{e}^{x+y}\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "325a719e-fd77-51b3-8a73-41ee2a5e0859"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b97760-1703-5852-afc4-69acd21e7cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424603} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd6afbc-8569-51f5-a569-dd388d648b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424605} {:answer/id #uuid "79d50006-6f06-56c3-bce2-b65f3e1eb21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424607} {:answer/id #uuid "d37fe04f-4a91-5f51-9524-f7477c2a7f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424612} {:answer/id #uuid "e17522ae-4622-53a9-bb60-7bec4bbcce91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdba31b-0f4f-583e-a52e-f0510ad8e3c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "515517e3-98f2-5a9f-ba03-8d3b47263ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-77258" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d4c2c86ed8506ff034141ac20a5a56587a80370384ceeaf08c66f2ce3e8a315.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(4,-2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(4,-2)$ to the point $B(4,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{8}{3},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the section of the curve $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i-\\sqrt{t}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 4.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=-\\sqrt{t}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j) \\\\  & =t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2})\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{4}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{4}^{3/2}}{3}-0)+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7063d477-7b30-59c1-824d-924e55d3b439" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b359e4-02cd-5e97-8b05-35ed5f867cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424602} {:answer/id #uuid "65eebd17-cd71-544b-a441-a52714eabfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424606} {:answer/id #uuid "97231ff5-005f-5e53-bec5-aa0b22f5882f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424610} {:answer/id #uuid "e35dbb22-7632-5914-b08b-f1a1a963fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424614} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b66872-c5ce-599c-8fa3-26dd57b225b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424615} :answer-field/id #uuid "d716774a-ff3b-5a8f-950b-7d77b8284d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "37832b23-49d2-5d4e-9f43-b03259f479e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-77145" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3c874f3c4564795a2ddcfb4c2896c1bc0ef1b4b7b3d8e8318f42d15643942d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $UVWU,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c29adf1a-cc66-5223-9397-edc496c85d37" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424624}}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424601}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163068} :activity/id #uuid "fdff7ccc-5a47-5748-b28e-bcf8de0682a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049674 :topic/id #uuid "09e82368-7656-5709-b72f-b4db605d6406" :topic/math-academy-id 3013} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163326 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the sample mean, denoted $\\overline{X},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nThe sample mean is a random variable whose probability distribution (also called the **sampling distribution**) has an expected value of $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\n \n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nLet's now find the variance of the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ Using the properties of variance for sums of independent random variables, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\text{Var}[\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+⋯+\\text{Var}[{X}_{n}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot ({\\sigma }^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}+⋯+{\\sigma }^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot n{\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important result:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}.$ What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\overline{X}$ is, it has mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58763c8a-23b3-55bb-9b5c-4781a3c271b4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6276 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0fb9bf51-5dff-5207-8c2c-54d18ba6afd9" :step/math-academy-id 38034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161672 :question/id #uuid "486f4292-2f50-5d89-be1a-b3c054b449e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10082"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bebcdc0f-dc9b-54ec-9cc5-49b2c046e8b7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050606}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435315}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance of a Sample Mean"} :step/id #uuid "0828c240-f63f-5c50-a2e8-eba11979ee4c" :step/math-academy-id 38036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163070 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162107 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161673 :question/id #uuid "41713dda-2195-5bd5-b0be-941b8188cb25" :question/math-academy-id "e-10083"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6eac4a2e-cfa8-5476-919e-fd3c26d01d75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491194}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "ae3ab16a-d830-544c-8331-c62d1505d7dd" :step/math-academy-id 38037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162108 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161674 :question/id #uuid "0f59a118-79ce-5746-8917-6f4092dcbc6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10084"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9453a0f1-029b-5436-94c1-47ee311f6e4d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418455} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491203}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Appropriate Sample Size"} :step/id #uuid "6c760552-8a9d-54b8-962b-87e6f849c3da" :step/math-academy-id 38040} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163333 :tutorial/content "The standard deviation of the sample mean is called the **standard error** of the sample mean and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}]\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}]}\n$$\n\nSo, if we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2,$ then the  standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\overline{X}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}} = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c2217692-f38f-597c-9295-cd25f1d4d026" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6300 :tutorial/title "The Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "2f745e7f-72c3-508a-96ad-ef71ea659d96" :step/math-academy-id 38149 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163070}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163334 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random sample ![](images/ff/ff40ddf134336ef15bcaf81b5b23aa8fb7c5ea13276e0f86fa6dde15841e0c5d.svg) of size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) from a population with mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) and variance ![](images/1a/1af459c3f0307649888db8b148eef968a9c5da6a77788c4ab7fe32d6ea30717e.svg) We know that the sample mean \n\n![](images/51/513b88bd8fa93336cc949758da79cac238f871b477d1682ab6b20635a4149361.svg)\n\nis an unbiased estimator of ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) \n\nHowever, it should be noted that selecting a single observation ![](images/3c/3c8b064868e0622effd6b83427f453ecdb3e033319c9e02969bb988b22ec7cb6.svg) to represent the whole could *also* be considered an unbiased estimator of the population mean. Recall that, by assumption\n\n![](images/e7/e7cc0631f5e6b060f0b9fc77dcac428c2b853d0e19be2e9b20e6e9f8d5d7aaad.svg)\n\nIn what sense is ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) a \"better\" estimator of ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) than, say, ![](images/c3/c3bb2b6f3769acd8b9c9ce9f929b5bc6c7ec840ab18503cf4c878a1fe8f8f41d.svg) The answer lies in their respective variances. \n\nAs the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) becomes larger and larger, the variance and standard error of ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) become smaller and smaller. It is easy to see that, as ![](images/f4/f4d85c2aac179db9322aef2478cfa080e9d602f8aeb8c0436f646f0e2321c139.svg)\n we have\n![](images/d5/d5676c70b4fcb7f68704ba0bb46fc262c7d8f764748e252aa989d6e82e88283b.svg)\n\nSo, when the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is large, there is less variance in the sampling distribution of ![](images/dc/dc01b2699b68bb8be585039efc249eaeeba5e4ad17a5c687a8811bf266cabc87.svg) Therefore, the larger the sample, the more likely it is that ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) will give good estimates for ![](images/98/9878f65a2daf16e1fbd2b0c4929e4407c4ddc406c147dab8a5f6b1a8ee25af33.svg) The individual sample elements do not have this property since ![](images/63/631e2156cfdd9541694e1238fb8c717e721e0ef452de4bb898beb2c4509abc6b.svg)\n\nThe key takeaway is that the sample mean ![](images/84/84269b1ed65c2a169ac4246065d27cbba714010309ddb03a65f7fa878b7b65ea.svg) becomes a better estimator for the corresponding population mean ![](images/75/75d2faaecd7646e08d87276399a471f8f713fbeda95e358e94410ac2e23b42a5.svg) as the sample size ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) increases." :tutorial/id #uuid "2fc54f89-7780-558c-8193-28d92a025bc8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6301 :tutorial/title "The Sample Mean and Large Samples"} :step/id #uuid "c79d4341-23ea-55d9-84aa-e073291790d1" :step/math-academy-id 38150 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163071}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sample Means" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162169}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425756} :activity/id #uuid "7fa56d66-b299-55c7-8a43-47f002ff063b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049674 :topic/id #uuid "09e82368-7656-5709-b72f-b4db605d6406" :topic/math-academy-id 3013} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210567c7-e894-5b02-9ee8-c6893172df38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.18\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425735} {:answer/id #uuid "60c93555-b34b-575f-95b2-f38de1599831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.15\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} {:answer/id #uuid "ca20eb7d-1ae8-5c45-ab85-1bae5f9c5811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.32\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425747} {:answer/id #uuid "cce28df6-b4e2-5293-9860-139a95e35caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.24\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425748} {:answer/id #uuid "cfed1752-e352-5a6b-9e2d-7dc6a36ff6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.33\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23ca134-e512-533c-83e5-e06d13cf4c5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bcb58811-e371-5576-bf66-5ecfddbfc1fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143202" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ of three nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $4+1=5$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm}\\,10\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot 5+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(20+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{5} \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{cm},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot {5}^{2}+{10}^{2})-{6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(200)-36 \\\\  & =40-36 \\\\  & =4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{4}{3}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}≈1.15\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "26fcd0c3-24b3-5951-9b42-4462bd2fac3d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425759}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425757 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116200b8-f0e0-5d54-986b-cdb0d1f93684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425732} {:answer/id #uuid "19e33d38-75d1-531e-92cf-f39947aea28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425734} {:answer/id #uuid "3e89daf0-19fa-53f4-b72d-31d6ed1621bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425737} {:answer/id #uuid "84db3eb9-187b-5476-8ae3-4fc97bb737af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425742} {:answer/id #uuid "b7436ec0-730a-5723-89b9-c51e09d25df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425734} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa84345-5063-5b45-a9a8-f4e3ac36fb05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "27d2a840-69cf-5325-b960-367fa82827bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-143424" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{8}$ is a sample of size $n=8$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =30$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=16,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=16$ and the sample size is $n=8,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{2}≈1.41.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3e79243e-67ec-56e0-9d13-5331e6398a52" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425760}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425758 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8420157b-408c-5305-9a9c-8bd7f4b5f5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159567} {:answer/id #uuid "cebb23ff-c448-538e-b197-5f1fefdfaf82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159568} {:answer/id #uuid "34924901-abbb-58af-a06c-4d9ff03048f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159569} {:answer/id #uuid "4e216592-6644-5ba7-91d9-81d6f47ad0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159570} {:answer/id #uuid "560505f7-e1a5-5ea3-b88a-4c5d794b42fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159571}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186159571} :answer-field/id #uuid "c12e791e-1ac8-53c0-861b-6e4d348b043d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e399cca8-8b5c-5ff9-b9d5-05eda26fea4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143498" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ employees is drawn from a population. The population has employees with two different working hours, $38\\,\\text{h/week}$ and $40\\,\\text{h/week},$ distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than  $0.5\\,\\text{h/week}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the employees in the population have working hours $38\\,\\text{h/week}$ and $40\\,\\text{h/week},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+1=4$ employees only with the following working hours:\n\n$$\n38\\,\\text{h/week},\\,38\\,\\text{h/week},\\,38\\,\\text{h/week},\\,40\\,\\text{h/week}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 38+40) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(114+40) \\\\  & =\\frac{154}{4} \\\\  & =38.5\\,\\text{h/week},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot {38}^{2}+{40}^{2})-{38.5}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(5\\,932)-1\\,482.25 \\\\  & =1\\,483-1\\,482.25 \\\\  & =0.75\\,{(\\text{h/week})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.75},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.5\\,\\text{h/week}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}} & <0.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.75}}{0.5} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{{0.5}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{0.25}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{3} \\\\ n & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "a723e053-b0fa-5616-b5a2-e66027d73c57"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2ebf1d-6b21-517e-8473-6b5520ced24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425730} {:answer/id #uuid "25a99cc8-8701-579f-b7ef-cb9e7b7f1e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425736} {:answer/id #uuid "55e8daa5-4432-5f7f-b7b9-25ac21b5f238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425738} {:answer/id #uuid "785ab3f2-459a-5180-8933-34aa9440618c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425740} {:answer/id #uuid "92d71196-a3a0-5ab1-b5f5-5721df71e75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425736} :answer-field/id #uuid "5943d631-1c0b-56a0-a90d-e9d31d030f54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "748a2779-e90d-59a2-9916-b0ad2a8182a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143423" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ is a sample of size $n=20$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =6$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=1$ and the sample size is $n=20,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{1}{20} \\\\  & =0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.05}≈0.22.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c77ffddd-aed2-56b6-b329-53374af11408" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425757}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425760 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425755 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7b64bf-b6ca-57e9-aaa1-181aed3d622b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "197" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425731} {:answer/id #uuid "160f3d12-5eab-5995-9f63-e81fe88512ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "241" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425733} {:answer/id #uuid "78a69e66-0db0-56b0-9e49-ac78e00b8276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "441" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425741} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2a8088-2557-53ce-b8f2-d4dcab799771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "194" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425743} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7b1ee7-ec01-58b0-9c87-f819883b152a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425745} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e022f4b-ff74-5796-8a0f-0137d56c7a63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d1b6912a-93b9-571b-8ea5-2d8ab50d8a7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143486" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ bushes is drawn from a population. The population has bushes of two different heights, $4\\,\\text{ft}$ and $5\\,\\text{ft},$ distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.01\\,\\text{ft}.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the bushes in the population have heights $4\\,\\text{ft}$ and $5\\,\\text{ft},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+2=5$ bushes only with the following heights:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\text{ft},\\,4\\,\\text{ft},\\,4\\,\\text{ft},\\,5\\,\\text{ft},\\,5\\,\\text{ft}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(12+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{22}{5} \\\\  & =4.4\\,\\text{ft},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot {4}^{2}+2\\cdot {5}^{2})-{4.4}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(98)-19.36 \\\\  & =19.6-19.36 \\\\  & =0.24\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.24},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.01\\,\\text{ft}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{n}} & <0.01 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.24}}{0.01} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.24}{{0.01}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{2\\,400} \\\\ n & >2\\,400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=2\\,401.$"} :step/id #uuid "fe9fb3ff-fe13-546b-ad08-eeb0688c4db2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425758}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sample Means" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425729}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447173} :activity/id #uuid "2f3ba771-5853-531e-b5df-7ce8360fb615" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049674 :topic/id #uuid "09e82368-7656-5709-b72f-b4db605d6406" :topic/math-academy-id 3013} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447169 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2ebf1d-6b21-517e-8473-6b5520ced24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425730} {:answer/id #uuid "25a99cc8-8701-579f-b7ef-cb9e7b7f1e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425736} {:answer/id #uuid "55e8daa5-4432-5f7f-b7b9-25ac21b5f238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425738} {:answer/id #uuid "785ab3f2-459a-5180-8933-34aa9440618c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425740} {:answer/id #uuid "92d71196-a3a0-5ab1-b5f5-5721df71e75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.71" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425736} :answer-field/id #uuid "5943d631-1c0b-56a0-a90d-e9d31d030f54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "748a2779-e90d-59a2-9916-b0ad2a8182a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143423" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ is a sample of size $n=20$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =6$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=1$ and the sample size is $n=20,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{1}{20} \\\\  & =0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.05}≈0.22.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2f448fd7-9340-59cd-a3ee-bfee3dc9c099" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447172}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447170 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52aca894-e775-54c1-8b43-216acbc28ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447162} {:answer/id #uuid "99113dff-3b5a-5c2f-a393-160c589b1310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "201" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447163} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3f329b-2d56-5c65-8fd5-ce307d766978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447164} {:answer/id #uuid "c6919965-008c-583a-804a-02b29893ad0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447165} {:answer/id #uuid "f7eb1986-1eeb-5a7e-94d4-f2aae2d2a16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "901" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447166}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447165} :answer-field/id #uuid "962adc96-285b-55db-b3b2-45bc705d2628" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74bfeec3-3f4e-5c4f-b7e4-18502ff50810" :question/math-academy-id "q-143443" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =100$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=25,$  determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.25.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{25}=5,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.25.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.25 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{5}{0.25} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >20 \\\\ n & >{20}^{2} \\\\ n & >400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=401.$"} :step/id #uuid "5815b438-b2dd-53fb-b220-9ae07c35776e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447171 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "353a41d3-498d-5a8a-a9e6-efadecfbdf97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418422} {:answer/id #uuid "51e57186-4049-585f-8889-b1eedaf9cb49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418426} {:answer/id #uuid "772d9248-19f9-5fe6-9b77-c1a0f57c290b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "62\\,501" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418430} {:answer/id #uuid "8a57d240-911f-55ae-81aa-4c9727b14650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418432} {:answer/id #uuid "bb42c329-8069-528c-b75c-f226cec7f9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418440}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418432} :answer-field/id #uuid "795337c1-be28-5aa3-ba9d-a058d2de96cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0c52d3d8-d8c0-58d1-b731-d5f1789535ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-143439" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =5$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=4,$  determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.02.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{4}=2,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.02.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{2}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >100 \\\\ n & >{100}^{2} \\\\ n & >10\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=10\\,001.$"} :step/id #uuid "86db7aa4-526f-594e-98bb-07bf1f02e96b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447170}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447172 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "116200b8-f0e0-5d54-986b-cdb0d1f93684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425732} {:answer/id #uuid "19e33d38-75d1-531e-92cf-f39947aea28c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425734} {:answer/id #uuid "3e89daf0-19fa-53f4-b72d-31d6ed1621bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425737} {:answer/id #uuid "84db3eb9-187b-5476-8ae3-4fc97bb737af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425742} {:answer/id #uuid "b7436ec0-730a-5723-89b9-c51e09d25df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425734} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fa84345-5063-5b45-a9a8-f4e3ac36fb05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "27d2a840-69cf-5325-b960-367fa82827bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-143424" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{8}$ is a sample of size $n=8$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =30$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=16,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=16$ and the sample size is $n=8,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{2}≈1.41.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "92bf9caa-0a99-5b2b-8d0b-624598682601" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447171}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447173 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d48c9f9b-3549-5764-b708-d84d1a81277e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159489} {:answer/id #uuid "078e2955-6c71-5d06-b3c7-66ce9b2711c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159490} {:answer/id #uuid "17f2bf08-271e-5210-9635-b7feef509a21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159491} {:answer/id #uuid "7709bcf7-4a06-5cb8-8170-55b19bc2fce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159492} {:answer/id #uuid "7793508a-e542-5e32-af73-86d3caa2d640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186159490} :answer-field/id #uuid "1673aac7-1c38-545e-83b9-0f96062e75d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "503fe913-6b66-5820-b334-367782412a2a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143197" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{100}$ is a sample of size $n=100$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =30$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=9,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=9$ and the sample size $n=100,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{9}{100} \\\\  & =0.09.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.09}=0.3.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e819b894-7ed2-5c6c-9756-eea6cdfe101a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447169}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sample Means" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447161}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491271} :activity/id #uuid "c64c48fd-6421-5002-b8ac-ec2858dbbea6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049674 :topic/id #uuid "09e82368-7656-5709-b72f-b4db605d6406" :topic/math-academy-id 3013} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491224 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d27b381-2583-5b7a-9fca-f1b985469a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490564} {:answer/id #uuid "285d902e-e585-5afb-b7d0-a096327f6bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490584} {:answer/id #uuid "720b4a42-214e-5e9a-9503-6877dcdc5e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490736} {:answer/id #uuid "88c5126e-12bf-54b2-b915-69e90cd55a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490783} {:answer/id #uuid "cfaa20be-1127-571b-9b08-97a73a594545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490903}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490736} :answer-field/id #uuid "a928408c-e2bb-5ac3-8926-8be8d60e417f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bbae1413-e0da-5920-9a81-9f35b978911e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143422" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ is a sample of size $n=50$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =12$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=4,$ calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=4$ and the sample size is $n=50,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{4}{50} \\\\  & =0.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.08}≈0.28.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "145bbf22-1749-5090-aadc-e3554ab59101" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2340fb78-3bc0-52f2-8490-70b953ad6bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490575} {:answer/id #uuid "5361feae-c64c-5ad9-9e88-dc19f7cade4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490670} {:answer/id #uuid "78e50466-a068-54f3-a9e1-1405af2707ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "201" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490751} {:answer/id #uuid "96643632-e50d-5725-b254-61e0b31e5d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,001" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490816} {:answer/id #uuid "e63fe34a-b9f7-5470-aaa5-176d56673f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "41" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490951}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490575} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aee0077-edc2-5b86-802c-50ab5c7a8951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9f4e58b8-0514-5aa4-9211-b553375a01af" :question/math-academy-id "q-143489" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ coins is drawn from a population. The population has coins of two different denominations, $$1$ and $$0.50,$ distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $$0.02.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coins in the population have denominations $$1$ and $$0.50,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+4=5$ coins only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$1,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+4\\cdot 0.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =$0.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({1}^{2}+4\\cdot {0.5}^{2})-{0.6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2)-0.36 \\\\  & =0.4-0.36 \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.04}=0.2,$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $$0.02.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{0.2}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >10 \\\\ n & >{10}^{2} \\\\ n & >100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=101.$"} :step/id #uuid "46254631-f53c-514a-8d4a-8cae395482d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491274}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210567c7-e894-5b02-9ee8-c6893172df38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.18\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425735} {:answer/id #uuid "60c93555-b34b-575f-95b2-f38de1599831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.15\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} {:answer/id #uuid "ca20eb7d-1ae8-5c45-ab85-1bae5f9c5811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.32\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425747} {:answer/id #uuid "cce28df6-b4e2-5293-9860-139a95e35caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.24\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425748} {:answer/id #uuid "cfed1752-e352-5a6b-9e2d-7dc6a36ff6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.33\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425739} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23ca134-e512-533c-83e5-e06d13cf4c5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bcb58811-e371-5576-bf66-5ecfddbfc1fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143202" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ of three nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Calculate the standard error of $\\overset{―}{X}.$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $5\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{10}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $4:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $4+1=5$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm},\\,5\\,\\text{cm}\\,10\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot 5+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(20+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{5} \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{cm},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(4\\cdot {5}^{2}+{10}^{2})-{6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(200)-36 \\\\  & =40-36 \\\\  & =4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{4}{3}\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}≈1.15\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} :step/id #uuid "e09f4a70-dbb4-5510-9c9f-f62ce6e6b9da" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491224}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491274 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00419cc9-1483-5e06-b0b3-661e4e54899f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490512} {:answer/id #uuid "47723024-b4a1-584b-a829-85dceb9329c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490643} {:answer/id #uuid "80b0e3c0-0da7-58eb-94d5-d78a9853d5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490770} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb60d40-47ca-586a-82af-614639d331fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490897} {:answer/id #uuid "e18c0630-b233-59ad-b582-a2188863f6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490936}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490897} :answer-field/id #uuid "c686f557-e13f-5945-8efd-5e2a53507af7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "18073722-b26c-57f1-b864-bfe7c648363b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143495" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ of $n$ shirts is drawn from a population. The population has shirts of two different sizes, $8$ and $10,$ distributed in the ratio $1:3.$ Determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the shirts in the population have sizes $8$ and $10,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:3.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+3=4$ shirts only with the following sizes:\n\n$$\n8,\\,10,\\,10,\\,10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8+3\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8+30) \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{4} \\\\  & =9.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({8}^{2}+3\\cdot {10}^{2})-{9.5}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(364)-90.25 \\\\  & =91-90.25 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.75},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.5.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}} & <0.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.75}}{0.5} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{{0.5}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{0.25}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{3} \\\\ n & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "f623e816-8a9f-53dc-ac9b-5d259ddbb546"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb4622f-90b6-5a96-a333-ac1ffe1c4ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "122" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490568} {:answer/id #uuid "7156e35a-a0cd-5c4a-9cfe-86803b267024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490734} {:answer/id #uuid "cad96efa-207b-59ed-9afb-6c27a870f1d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,305" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490895} {:answer/id #uuid "db47dba0-0807-5e27-ac1c-6bd558d9926c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490927} {:answer/id #uuid "dc1775bd-6de6-57e4-b281-5dc5531234f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490928}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490928} :answer-field/id #uuid "a38d7fe4-d3d9-5f07-8a46-a68180a20afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df86b42c-6e72-556f-b425-3d68c35d4d8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143440" :question/problem "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with mean $\\mu =24$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$  determine the smallest sample size required so that the standard error of the mean is less than $0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{1}=1,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.5.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >2 \\\\ n & >{2}^{2} \\\\ n & >4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=5.$"} :step/id #uuid "faa8d334-a6c2-5d81-9e07-13cab6f2da50" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491233}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sample Means" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490511}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163007} :activity/id #uuid "fa26629c-b4dd-57db-9661-3b43692ea236" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050535 :topic/id #uuid "51d67380-dd70-5f91-a192-de8ab2b56b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163291 :tutorial/content "We can easily extend the notion of conditional probability distributions for discrete random variables to continuous random variables.\n\nFor continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$, the **conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y = y$** is given by\n\n$$\nf_{X \\mid Y} (x \\mid y) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_Y (y)}, \\qquad f_Y(y) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nand the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X = x$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{Y \\mid X} (y \\mid x) =\\dfrac{f(x, y)}{f_X (x)}, \\qquad f_X(x) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions are analogous to those for discrete random variables.\n\nTo compute probabilities, we work directly with these conditional PDFs. For example, to calculate the **conditional probability of $X$ given $Y$,** we use\n\n$$\nP(X \\in A \\mid Y = y) =\\int_A f_{X \\mid Y} (x \\mid y)\\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nIn general, the conditional distribution of $X$ given $Y$ does not equal the conditional distribution of $Y$ given $X$. That is\n\n$$\nf_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y) \\ne f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)\n$$\n\nFinally, if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\nf_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)=f_X(x),\\qquad f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)=f_Y(y)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4e66c559-ad43-5152-a04e-7aa8b01374ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5625 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc469a65-06a8-5cb9-9eed-1c3d9f00c4c2" :step/math-academy-id 34590 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162112 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161678 :question/id #uuid "29e739b1-8745-517f-9011-d1761364c4c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10099"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "444ad045-3c7b-5668-bc3a-e3cb49a5b6f2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049553}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162393}] :knowledge-point/title "Working With the Definition of a Conditional PDF"} :step/id #uuid "5d645e06-ba3d-5245-bff3-396e4a4a04e4" :step/math-academy-id 38091 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162113 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161679 :question/id #uuid "3439786b-905b-5642-89d1-61b0345f79e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10100"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "184d9e09-3335-5617-8989-e5ab08ca04e7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413415}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Conditional Probability"} :step/id #uuid "f08e3c7b-ff47-5108-9d4d-3471aa625605" :step/math-academy-id 38092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162114 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161680 :question/id #uuid "f1c9e136-57b3-59ae-90e0-b28921be44ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-10101"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb3b4b6a-3a24-5079-bf33-ed1c5576ffa7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162396}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional PDF"} :step/id #uuid "ccc00629-902d-501f-86c2-7072c1aad6a6" :step/math-academy-id 38093 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163079}}] :activity/title "Conditional Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162176}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413418} :activity/id #uuid "8d71dbb8-3f6b-51a3-b4b2-a5b2c8b23a9b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050535 :topic/id #uuid "51d67380-dd70-5f91-a192-de8ab2b56b57" :topic/math-academy-id 3022} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0232ff5b-0d05-5778-88a0-ff0af66fc111" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413397} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7ebaa8-7f99-5427-8a2f-237d9f6e4410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413398} {:answer/id #uuid "291ffaa1-af34-572c-8208-632d599387b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413399} {:answer/id #uuid "2bab8694-81fe-5f61-ab63-c2f3224466f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413401} {:answer/id #uuid "cb999cce-827d-57cd-aed2-f09c922819e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413398} :answer-field/id #uuid "76914fbd-7e46-5e1e-a2ea-82b62a7c5f3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6fd86b7a-aa64-5ea3-a90b-7af5da2353f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143574" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}8xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{2}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ is the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}8xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8y{\\int }_{0}^{y}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8y{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{y} \\\\  & =4y({y}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)={\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{8xy}{4{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{2}{3}) & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y=\\frac{2}{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}\\frac{2x}{{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\int }_{1/4}^{1/2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{[{x}^{2}]}_{1/4}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}(\\frac{3}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9476e3d9-7d05-54ea-ba2e-44132f1512b5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413417}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b4b973f-cb9d-5e7e-8beb-4c556f2b875c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413400} {:answer/id #uuid "6a95ee3c-3035-5909-a68e-b2eac9893340" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5y(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413402} {:answer/id #uuid "726964d4-b990-596e-9009-e83d6d208a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(2x+3y)(y+1)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413403} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e4876d-bca4-5991-b345-bf1e1f8f35e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(y+1)}{5(2x+3y)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413406} {:answer/id #uuid "e77674d9-5e17-517e-8f5b-956421030e91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413409}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413400} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b4844fd-48c8-5187-86c9-fcbf91d0bc32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "228badef-897c-5f4a-80d9-699c2c689782" :question/math-academy-id "q-143634" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the marginal density function for $Y,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\frac{4xy}{5}+\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{2}y}{5}+\\frac{6x{y}^{2}}{5}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8y}{5}+\\frac{12{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{2y}{5}-\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{6y}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $X$ given $Y=y$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{Y}(y)$ is the marginal density function for $Y.$\n\nNow, for $1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2y(2x+3y)}{5})}{(\\frac{6y(y+1)}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3y}{3(y+1)}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4e2ce8c-ef99-5939-91ca-5637d283ed7e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413418 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76dedc47-c082-5f11-bb91-f5c53a5feef0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413404} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e703b0-4bd0-5a40-af83-db0c989a5aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413405} {:answer/id #uuid "db303bfa-69cf-5cf1-86a5-b4a2624334a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413408} {:answer/id #uuid "efd7c23f-d7a0-5417-baaf-c56cd1849f93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413410} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf61311-9fef-553a-a566-6bcfee2063e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413404} :answer-field/id #uuid "7133b6f3-994d-586b-8bc7-52facdb8edd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "990850d6-75f9-5e72-98e4-4c732056938a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143461" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}24xy, & \\,0<x<1,0<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that \n\n$$\nP(Y\\in A\\,|\\,X=x)={\\int }_{A}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ is the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x.$ \n\nUsing the formula for the marginal density function for $X,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1-x}24xy\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24x{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =24x{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1-x} \\\\  & =12x(1-x)^{2}-0 \\\\  & =12x(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)={\\begin{aligned}12x(x-1)^{2}, & \\,0<x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow recall that the conditional probability density function of $Y$ given $X=x$ for two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $f(x,y)$ is the joint probability density function for $X$ and $Y,$ and\n\n- ${f}_{X}(x)$ is the marginal density function for $X.$\n\nNow, for $0<x<1,0<y<1-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x) & =\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{24xy}{12x(x-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{(x-1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor other values of $x$ and $y,$ the function $f(x,y)$ equals $0.$ As a result, ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ will be equal to $0$ too.\n\nTherefore, the conditional probability density function is the following:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{(x-1)^{2}}, & \\,0<x<1,0<y<1-x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute our probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<X\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}) & =P(Y<\\frac{1}{3}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x=\\frac{1}{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/3}\\frac{2y}{{(\\frac{1}{3}-1)}^{2}}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(\\frac{1}{18}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cd604f93-e377-5bb5-97aa-db0cfd4f422a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413416}}] :activity/title "Conditional Distributions for Continuous Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413396}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180496} :activity/id #uuid "3e766c6c-8044-509d-9e8e-e4e4bbddcfe5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050773 :topic/id #uuid "659072cc-8b2c-5e8d-b6a6-88321581f47f" :topic/math-academy-id 3713} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179602 :question/id #uuid "6c8b136c-7944-599f-9399-19641212fcbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9537"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049147}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259835} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501169}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "3bb7385f-5c70-5eed-b8c4-e230a20a67a7" :step/math-academy-id 35728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180495}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180495 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179741 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179603 :question/id #uuid "16605c7c-c238-51ef-84e6-d5b5d58e1539" :question/math-academy-id "e-9539"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259808} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259810} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455447}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Circulation"} :step/id #uuid "8aff9a77-6b15-506d-8bc4-aca89e0ff920" :step/math-academy-id 35730 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180496 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180719 :tutorial/content "Given a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and a path $C$ defined by $\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b],$ the line integral \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\ngives the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle from the initial point $\\mathbf{r}(a)$ to the terminal point $\\mathbf{r}(b)$ on the curve.\n\nNow suppose that $C$ is a *closed* curve. The **circulation** of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C,$ denoted using the Greek letter $\\Gamma$ (pronounced \"gamma\"), is given by the line integral\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nRemember that the notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ simply reminds us that we're integrating along a closed path.\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ gives the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along the entire closed path. We can also think of $\\Gamma$ as a measure of how much $\\mathbf F$ circulates around $C.$\n\nLet's build some intuition by considering some examples. \n\n- Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ and the closed curve $C$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction, shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e9de4e3734be4f7d8f59f6d54d0ed92c51db3a0beb8277f4b450531b7dab332.png)\n\nIt's clear from the picture that $\\mathbf{F}$ *assists* the motion of a particle as it moves counterclockwise along the path. Therefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r >    0.\n$$\n\n- Now imagine that $C$ is traversed in the clockwise direction.\n\n![](images/16/16dd701d51e3b43714b58993f66e42359289f557ef86fd9091ab6b8bba7041e7.png)\n\nWe see that $\\mathbf F$ works *against* the particle's motion as it moves clockwise along the path. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, consider the vector field $\\mathbf{G}$ and the closed curve $C$ traversed in the clockwise direction, shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8bf269cecceeefc3e81b868a4fc4c54127f00432145782b24719bb49552868c7.png)\n\nSince the force $\\mathbf G$ acts perpendicular to the particle's motion as it moves around the path, it neither helps nor hinders its motion. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf G\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4446c197-397c-5e41-a7a9-22d57126ffae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5833 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2145b39b-47ee-5ae2-ad16-55a2719cc2e0" :step/math-academy-id 35732 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180499 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179605 :question/id #uuid "1e15ae93-0a63-506a-a0ae-b15e035f0494" :question/math-academy-id "e-9543"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049150}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259836} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415314}] :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "3bb98ffd-856f-5d48-bd6b-55f3885cdca8" :step/math-academy-id 35749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180721 :tutorial/content "The fundamental theorem of line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is a piecewise-smooth curve $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and $f$ is differentiable function such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ \n\nNow, if $C$ is a closed path that's traversed once, then $\\mathbf r(a) = \\mathbf r(b),$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circulation of a conservative vector field around *any* closed path equals zero." :tutorial/id #uuid "5bc343b6-5187-5353-bb7b-23db12eaa417" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5836 :tutorial/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"} :step/id #uuid "345a9b9c-ae65-5c52-85e2-8ffa7905b813" :step/math-academy-id 35756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180499}}] :activity/title "Circulation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179865}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455518} :activity/id #uuid "129cb966-e09b-519a-a368-62dfaa636adb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050773 :topic/id #uuid "659072cc-8b2c-5e8d-b6a6-88321581f47f" :topic/math-academy-id 3713} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7781bfa2-360a-52f2-b8a7-5fd12172c6fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455124} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad5e1c3-fe87-52c4-9343-a5e560de27a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455128} {:answer/id #uuid "9a55b0b7-ffd1-54c4-8119-7e1a1a488949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455185} {:answer/id #uuid "b6715a23-2351-5f62-8ba5-ef943e459d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455223} {:answer/id #uuid "c19b819d-c541-51b6-a6e0-7dae5f0106b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455223} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd93af99-5bab-55c2-8a85-e0d5efec7517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d78a254d-adb2-5551-a797-0a608524ca4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-125669" :question/problem "![](images/36/36ebce64036b7661c81aa6525c407f698375544532985175cc465ddc3ff4f5a8.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/c4/c498ddeae8ba4325a4a1dfc0840520c4718bb50a0600970405374dde933e1b1d.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/74/740429a29b5eb4b72bbfae11e08c9ac272e72e31d395b4dad2602beb4d4ba40a.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "23ee9455-766f-5586-95a2-a9ed8dfe87ce"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455518 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b7d45a6-b7b5-5b6e-a82c-a1748d83e603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454946} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4f36c3-2d0c-50c9-a36d-272fd914e84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455133} {:answer/id #uuid "c26f1bdf-26e0-5acd-bbc9-1a8e6be78f5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455239} {:answer/id #uuid "c3275529-012a-5a31-82a4-2f28fcd1acb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455240} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6f64d6-184a-5394-8a28-a8c004572db7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455240} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbf611c1-7a64-56ff-a8ce-599f171848cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "442b3fed-648b-5cc8-977b-6700ae26738b" :question/math-academy-id "q-125482" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "40211c38-7902-5a30-be18-54ba1e524e35" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455523}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455519 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40242699-9a76-5769-ad29-253e8f600b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455010} {:answer/id #uuid "63642342-0ea8-5389-ae31-2bce908c8ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455082} {:answer/id #uuid "90199dc9-7e02-5cd1-bdd7-df4867251c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455163} {:answer/id #uuid "97eeaf90-7d49-5a72-b321-ee76bbb6e4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455176} {:answer/id #uuid "c097de0b-9eac-5b4e-97f4-50838319facf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455176} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2afbdbe-c2b8-5f8b-8cd1-c52860a3b3d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "afd2a4b0-18e7-5bc4-9563-64bdaef16530" :question/math-academy-id "q-125666" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d042734a8c7483c54795cd6ec8527b43f398cb77487d054a36b27d704f27a318.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/f8/f8268167bb50f9d03cb38a3d505fa6b40003e307b149356089f5b0dc3dd32110.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a83934aa58040b4eb2bb957d1d12caba676e306e41086480d064935eab9f1c59.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "48918b6d-e2d1-57bd-906d-d8cb6cd2a9dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455533}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455485 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "029ef420-9eaa-5c6d-8287-3ef33710e976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454907} {:answer/id #uuid "3af01276-3947-5c77-abb0-001b7f9268cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455000} {:answer/id #uuid "74a31f1c-47e3-545d-a815-1b80644ceb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455118} {:answer/id #uuid "c134f711-db33-5b9e-bc96-94b44710886e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455236} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1d9b86-ba57-557d-9af4-ca1029204f80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455000} :answer-field/id #uuid "07b21c95-dc62-58a4-b763-c93eafdfda09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9318e5b-e833-584c-92bb-558dab7e94de" :question/math-academy-id "q-125640" :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1883ee3a3a99d43109bae2d2093aef35548dde9753cfb7dc19dfd77a104ec83.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/85/8595904122fac3431fc959b2fa4aa5a3062bd1bffd1a391a0861fa12f2498054.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1f31f2b6eefddedc46b8e845703904c0b4566963e64a644f7a4ab32bb7240c3.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "79347290-b94d-53e8-a6ae-104c05d725cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455519}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455533 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455463 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f5e806-242d-5b1a-8886-66c2da17be28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454981} {:answer/id #uuid "5b31d58a-98fe-5653-8b2e-f0931877d07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455066} {:answer/id #uuid "708e66ed-fb9c-572e-9876-d3e2f4e3957c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455113} {:answer/id #uuid "814fc88c-5be7-5618-be65-2f99b06af468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455137} {:answer/id #uuid "e34fc7a8-8d96-5fc5-a347-b84cc10b6757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455066} :answer-field/id #uuid "82c6016b-0343-53a1-a3ae-36d3278711ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186455377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "674f4764-9a63-51ae-b803-b7fdf438bb38" :question/math-academy-id "q-125667" :question/problem "![](images/13/13e4c6a06a44099024ce91ef6c9ed4679af037c2e794351597a4d604bf39dc6f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nII. ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nIII. ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/63/6371bb04d33719ee18c65a92354e84be0e9077e780eac9559dcb7ec42deb8d99.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/0b/0bd9476fa9b19f04b40fbb9cca2986ed90495aa134307606622ef91070af19a7.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "fa7bb3f5-8a01-54f6-a7fb-3948ae86282d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455515}}] :activity/title "Circulation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454899}]}
